---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/what-is-a-regular-expression.html">
  <aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
  <div class="wrap article">
    <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Developer basics</p>
    <h1>What Is a Text Pattern? A Plain Guide</h1>
    <p class="lead">A pattern is a short description of text, such as three digits, a dash and then four digits. Programmers use patterns to search and check text. This guide explains the basics in everyday terms.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>A pattern for text</h2>
<p>A pattern describes what text should look like, rather than the exact words. For example, the pattern for a phone number might say: three digits, a dash, then four digits. Any text that fits that shape matches.</p>
<h2>The main parts</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>A letter or number</strong> matches itself.</li>
<li><strong>A digit</strong> (written as \d) matches any number from 0 to 9.</li>
<li><strong>A count</strong> such as &#123;3&#125; means exactly that many of the item before it.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Where patterns are used</h2>
<p>Programmers use patterns to check that an email address looks right, to find every date in a document, or to tidy up text. Many word processors and search tools support simple patterns too.</p>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>A pattern for a phone number with a dash: three digits, a dash, then four digits. In the sentence 'Call 555-1234 today', the pattern finds 555-1234.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>What is the difference between greedy and lazy matching?</summary><p>A greedy pattern takes as much text as it can. A lazy one takes as little as possible. Add a question mark to make a pattern lazy.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What do the letters after a pattern mean?</summary><p>They are options. 'g' finds every match, and 'i' ignores capital letters.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How do I search for a real full stop?</summary><p>Put a backslash in front of it, so the pattern looks for a dot rather than any character.</p></details>
      </section>
